{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Research a stable O(lin log lin) sort to replace bubble_sort, the clearest remaining asymptotic bottleneck. Preserve the current stable ascending order for ordinary doubles (including equal values and signed zero), since median and mode depend on it. Handle NaNs by retaining the original sort path for affected columns; if scratch allocation fails, fall back rather than fail. Use scratch safely if combined with column-parallel work. Keep the input layout, arithmetic order, metrics, and output unchanged; retain the change only if correctness holds and measured time improves.\",\"B\":\"Refine mode calculation only; A owns sorting. On sorted columns, use a no-allocation run-length scan, updating the winner only for a strictly longer run so ties select the first sorted value and all-unique columns return -1. If a NaN is encountered, fall back to an equality-count scan that does not rely on uninitialized scratch. The existing run-length candidate measured 0.007402 s versus the 0.009463 s baseline, so use it as a measured lead, but recheck correctness and timing with other changes.\",\"C\":\"Research generic OpenMP worksharing across independent columns, preserving each column's serial arithmetic and accumulation order. The ARM64 target has four cores; cap the team at four, use static scheduling, avoid races, and keep a generic size guard to limit team overhead. The measured column-loop candidate at 0.007164 s was the fastest observed (versus 0.007231 s for a combined per-column worker); use it as a reference, not proof that further parallel changes help. Re-measure each variant and retain only correct improvements.\",\"G\":\"Integrate A's sort, B's mode, and C's parallel work only where their implementations are compatible. Start from the best measured standalone reference (C, 0.007164 s), then measure C+A, C+B, A+B, and the full combination; do not assume gains add. If sorting changes from quadratic to O(lin log lin), reassess C's parallel size guard generically rather than copying a threshold calibrated for bubble sort. Check the required seven output lines and correctness, preserve all-input behavior and output formatting, and retain a combined change only if its own measurements improve.\",\"analysis\":\"The official problem computes seven per-column statistics for double inputs, with sorting used before median and mode. The source has quadratic bubble sorting and quadratic mode counting, while columns are independent. Automatic measurements show the baseline at 0.009463 s; the best candidate is C's column parallelism at 0.007164 s, followed by C's combined worker at 0.007231 s, B's run-length mode at 0.007402 s, and A's mode variant at 0.007526 s. The mean/harmonic fusion measured 0.009583 s, slower than baseline, so do not prioritize it. These are standalone timings, not evidence of additive gains. Preserve defined mode tie behavior and deterministic counting; baseline's mode scratch was uninitialized.\"}"}}
